WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:04.710
Well, it's finally here: the trailer for the
final installment of the Skywalker Saga.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.800
And with the trailer we finally get a title:
The Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:12.550
Considering that Luke Skywalker seemed to
buy the moisture farm at the end of The Last

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.330
Jedi, we can't help but wonder just which
Skywalker is about to rise.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:19.550
We can only guess at what some of the scenes
in this trailer might mean.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.609
But rest assured - a lack of actual knowledge
never stopped us from speculating wildly about

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:24.620
trailers before.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.119
Crack open your Wookieepedias, and dust off
your holocrons - here are the small details

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:33.140
you might've missed in the first trailer for
Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:39.080
After two mainline movies' worth of fans constantly
wondering where smooth operator Lando Calrissian,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.260
played by BIlly Dee Williams, might be hiding,
the teaser finally confirmed that he'll return

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:44.800
in The Rise of Skywalker.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.700
And he's right back where fans last saw him
36 years ago at the end of Return of the Jedi,

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.640
sitting in the pilot's seat of the Millennium
Falcon and having a grand old time.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.250
His co-pilot this time out isn't rebel smuggler
Nien Nunb - who incidentally is still active

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.520
in the Resistance movement.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.690
Instead, it's none other than Chewbacca accompanying
Lando on a jump through hyperspace.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:04.200
Where are they headed?

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.960
A very brief shot of a battle scene looks
like it may take place in Lando's old stomping

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.700
grounds, Cloud City.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.080
"Lando!"

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.700
"Lando system?"

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:16.060
"Lando's not a system.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.520
He's a man."

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.240
Of course, fans have seen Lando in a movie
since Jedi: 2018's Solo: A Star Wars Story,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.140
where he was portrayed by Donald Glover instead
of Williams.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.950
Interestingly, Williams seems to be wearing
the same, or at least a very similar, yellow-and-black

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.160
costume as the one Glover wore in the prequel.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.920
Perhaps he's trying to recapture the swashbuckling
days of his youth?

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.540
Or maybe it's just so newer fans will recognize
him.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.340
Arguably the most touching moment in the first
teaser for The Rise of Skywalker is the brief

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.299
shot of Leia and Rey embracing in a forest-like
setting.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:51.420
A single tear runs down's Rey’s cheek as
Luke's voiceover intones, "We'll always be

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:52.080
with you."

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:56.540
While the in-universe context for this heartfelt
hug is still unknown, we do know that scenes

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:00.970
featuring the late Carrie Fisher in The Rise
of Skywalker are actually unused clips from

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:02.250
The Force Awakens.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:07.369
Much to the relief of Fisher fans worldwide,
Lucasfilm announced in July 2018 that her

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:13.160
character would not be controversially resurrected
via CGI, like Peter Cushing's Grand Moff Tarkin

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.420
in 2016's Rogue One.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.190
Instead, Abrams and his team would find a
way to work with a number of scenes that had

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:22.790
gone unused from Episode VII, as director
J.J. Abrams told the crowd at the Star Wars

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:24.900
Celebration 2019 in Chicago.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.319
"The idea of having a CG character was off
the table.

00:02:28.319 --> 00:02:30.230
We never even wanted to try.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.230
And the idea of saying, 'Well, what if we
could actually write scenes around her?'

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.230
So it would be her performance.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:35.860
She's in the movie."

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.300
So while it's still unclear why, exactly,
Leia and Rey are sharing this particular moment,

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.500
we know that it's not just a tacked-on deleted
scene.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.860
Whatever it was that prompted this embrace
in the Episode VII script changed the course

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:51.709
of Episode IX's story, one way or another,
meaning Fisher had a posthumous influence

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:56.160
on the direction of the Star Wars universe
- a fitting tribute to the legendary Leia.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:01.200
There's one moment in particular that has
hyperventilating fans reaching for their inhalers:

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.340
the moment where we see Kylo Ren's iconic
helmet being carefully pieced back together

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.380
by a mysterious - and hairy - figure.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.920
In The Force Awakens, that mask was in many
ways the key to who Kylo Ren is - or wanted

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:12.620
to be, at least.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:16.540
Unlike his grandfather, Darth Vader, Kylo
Ren didn't need the mask.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.280
But he chose to wear it as an outward symbol
of the person he was willing himself to become.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.780
In The Last Jedi, though, Kylo Ren smashed
the mask in a rage.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:25.160
That too, was symbolic.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.000
His whole character arc in The Last Jedi saw
him questioning his decision, and questioning

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:30.620
who he was.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.400
Rey tried to redeem him by tempting him back
to the light side, and he wavered.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.280
Some part of him was still Ben Solo.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.720
But in the end, he chose again to embrace
the dark side.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.900
So it would only be fitting if he once again
donned the mask: he knows who he is now, and

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.000
it's not Ben Solo.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.320
It's Kylo Ren, the inheritor of Darth Vader's
legacy - and the mask is an outward symbol

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.180
of who he's finally chosen to be.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:55.420
Beyond Rey's prequel-style feat of Force parkour,
the biggest money shot in the Rise of Skywalker

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.251
teaser is the shot of our next generation
heroes paying a visit to the wreckage of a

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.420
massive Death Star.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.080
You may be familiar with these.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.750
The question lingering over this shot is:
which Death Star is this?

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.470
In the series so far, we've seen only two
super-weapons of this particular design: one

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:14.130
in the first Star Wars, which was blown up
at the Battle of Yavin, and another in Return

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:18.620
of the Jedi, which was blown up with the Emperor
on board above the forest moon of Endor.

00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.250
Recency bias and the Emperor's well-timed
cackle in the teaser suggest that this wreckage

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:24.770
is of the second Death Star.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:29.330
But the surroundings - misty and unforested
- look less like Endor, and possibly more

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.440
like Yavin IV, where the original Rebels first
made their base.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.120
Of course, this being Star Wars, this well
could be a third, fourth, or sixty-sixth giant

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.500
super-weapon that we never got to see on screen
before.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.700
The most important thing is that it looks
cool, and in that case - hey, mission accomplished.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:48.740
Way back at the end of Star Wars: A New Hope,
the closing scene was Princess Leia awarding

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:49.940
medals to Han and Luke.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.810
We never found out the significance of those
medals or what they represent - outside of

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.710
being given for "excellence in Death Star
destruction."

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:01.460
And we never saw them ever again, until the
teaser for Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:06.310
At about the 1:33 mark, we see a pair of older-looking
hands cradling one of the medals that belonged

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.180
to Luke and Han.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.810
While we can't see whose hands they are, it
doesn't seem like much of a stretch that they

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.520
might belong to General Leia.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.280
We also can't know if this is Luke or Han's
medal, though either one would make a potent

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.800
memento for Leia to be holding during the
Skywalker Saga's epic conclusion.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.330
But let's take an educated guess: with all
the footage we see of a desert locale, the

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:28.840
voiceover provided by Mark Hamil, and the
title "The Rise of Skywalker," there's a good

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.350
chance that Luke's medal might play an instrumental
part in whatever Skywalker will be rising

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.340
by the end of this film.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.700
At the very end of the trailer, audiences
were treated to one final tease before the

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:39.400
title.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.240
The screen cuts to black, and an ominous laugh
plays over the darkness.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.080
(laughing)

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.960
Long-time fans might have recognized that
cackle as one that sounds eerily similar to

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.380
Emperor Palpatine's.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:53.880
How?

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.740
Okay, stop us if you've heard this one: A
long time ago, in a galaxy far away, Palpatine

00:05:58.751 --> 00:06:00.759
fell down a big hole and blew up.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.360
The end.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.140
That seems like a pretty serious injury to
come back from.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.160
But as multiple films have proven, nobody
in Star Wars is ever truly dead, if the narrative

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:09.920
calls for it.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.860
If that is in fact Palpatine's laugh - and
actor Ian McDiarmid's appearance at Star Wars

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:19.251
Celebration just after the trailer's debut
seems to indicate that it is - there are a

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.200
few possibilities for what it could mean for
Episode IX.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:23.800
First, he's back!

00:06:24.169 --> 00:06:28.900
With Snoke out of the picture, the upcoming
film will need a big baddie to raise the stakes.

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:33.490
But we also have Kylo Ren seemingly stepping
into that role, so another possibility is

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.150
that the laugh comes from an old hologram
of Palpatine.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:40.630
As the 2015 comic Shattered Empire showed,
the guy wasn't above giving orders after his

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:42.120
death via holograms.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.660
Maybe Rey and the gang discover an old recording
in that beat-up Death Star.

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:47.900
Or, hey - it could be a big ol' misdirect.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:49.600
We'll have to wait to find out.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.900
The first minute or so of the trailer shows
Rey going it alone against what seems to be

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.820
Kylo Ren's Tie Interceptor.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:01.020
While the epic-looking action scene is tense
and exciting enough, what's really interesting

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.470
is the setting - Rey is in the desert, and
that might mean some big, big things.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:09.699
In fact, several scenes in the trailer seem
to take place in a rocky, desert-like setting

00:07:09.699 --> 00:07:13.930
- like the one featuring Finn and Poe on a
rocky outcropping, and the explosive speeder

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:16.280
chase that has strong echoes of Return of
the Jedi.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.040
Unless you're new to this whole Star Wars
thing, you may know that our heroes have made

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.050
quite a few trips to the desert - both in
the movies, and in the TV shows.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:28.319
We've seen the twin-sunned Tatooine several
times throughout both the prequel trilogy

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:32.510
as well as the original trilogy, while The
Force Awakens switched things up by introducing

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:33.820
us to Rey on Jakku.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:38.300
Meanwhile, Star Wars Rebels, which details
the events before A New Hope, spends a lot

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.900
of time on the rocky, sandy planet of Lothal.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.660
So where do these new scenes take place?

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:47.190
Again, it's impossible to say for now, but
let's follow the several Skywalker clues and

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:51.150
guess that it's one last trip back to Tatooine
- the planet where this whole Star Wars mess

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.600
started - before we close the book on this
pesky family for good.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:59.760
Not much is known about the latest droid to
appear in the Star Wars universe - and, soon

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.080
enough, on Christmas 2019 shopping lists.

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:07.140
But we do know that this unicycle with a megaphone
for a head is named D.O., as confirmed by

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:13.520
The Rise of Skywalker director J.J. Abrams
at the Star Wars Celebration 2019 in Chicago.

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.720
Introduced during the panel as BB-8's "new
friend," D.O. was met with cheers and plenty

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:20.700
of "Awwws" as he appeared at the feet of the
panelists.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:25.280
As for D.O.'s appearance in the teaser trailer,
he's confined to just two shots.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:30.600
He first pops up tilting his head adorably
alongside BB-8, and then once more with the

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.240
core group of resistance heroes on what could
be Endor.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:37.619
D.O.'s origin is unclear, but what is clear
is that he's going to make a lot of children

00:08:37.619 --> 00:08:41.959
happy - and likely upset Star Wars diehards
who don't care for the cuter side of the galaxy

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:43.100
far, far away.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.620
Movie teasers tend to do exactly what they
say on the tin, offering up fleeting images

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:49.809
and scarce hints over real substance.

00:08:49.809 --> 00:08:53.480
The real takeaway from the Episode IX teaser
isn't the footage.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:57.740
It's the movie's title, The Rise of Skywalker
- a kind of off-the-wall choice.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:02.449
In practice, the title signals a hard commitment
to referencing the nine Star Wars episodes

00:09:02.449 --> 00:09:06.800
as the "Skywalker Saga" - a name we won't
know the full significance of until the series

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:07.640
is all wrapped up.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.920
For years, the name Skywalker has referred
to just Anakin and Luke, though if you ask

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.060
Yoda, Leia can get thrown in there too.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:17.200
"That boy is our last hope."

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.460
"No - there is another."

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.879
But the Rise of Skywalker title feels like
it elevates the name above and beyond reference

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.079
to one mere family.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:31.080
At the end of The Last Jedi, both Luke Skywalker
and the Jedi Order are effectively dead.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.700
Rey, a powerful Force wielder, is left with
the option to revive the Jedi - or replace

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:36.200
them.

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:40.520
Perhaps Skywalker isn't a name anymore, but
rather a title for a new kind of warrior monk.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.960
If that's the case, then Episode IX follows
naming conventions set by the previous two

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:50.060
trilogies - Revenge of the Sith, Return of
the Jedi, and now, The Rise of Skywalker.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:53.960
It's an idea that has a chance of sounding
silly if the movie comes out and doesn't go

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:54.460
with it.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:56.340
But for now, right after the trailer's dropped?

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:00.400
Absent any other information, it feels like
a theory we can get behind.

